---
import { cafeImage } from "../data/images"
---

<section class="relative bg-inverted py-section" aria-labelledby="cafe-heading">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-landing px-container-x sm:px-8 xl:px-14">
    <p
      class="reveal mb-6 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-inverted-foreground/50"
    >
      Cafe Budapest
    </p>

    <h2
      id="cafe-heading"
      class="reveal max-w-heading font-display text-headline-1 font-bold leading-[1.05] text-inverted-foreground"
    >
      The Conversations That Built a City
    </h2>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 sm:mt-16 lg:grid-cols-5 lg:gap-16">
      <!-- Photo -->
      <figure class="reveal lg:col-span-2">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-sm">
          <img
            src={`${cafeImage.src}&w=800&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80`}
            srcset={`${cafeImage.src}&w=400&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 400w, ${cafeImage.src}&w=600&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 600w, ${cafeImage.src}&w=800&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80 800w`}
            sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1024px) 90vw, 400px"
            alt={cafeImage.alt}
            title={`Photo by ${cafeImage.author} on Unsplash`}
            width={cafeImage.width}
            height={cafeImage.height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="photo-tone aspect-[3/4] w-full object-cover"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-xs text-inverted-foreground/40">
          Photo by <a
            href={cafeImage.authorUrl}
            class="underline hover:text-inverted-foreground/60"
            rel="noopener">{cafeImage.author}</a
          >
          on <a
            href={cafeImage.unsplashUrl}
            class="underline hover:text-inverted-foreground/60"
            rel="noopener">Unsplash</a
          >
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- Text content -->
      <div class="flex flex-col justify-center lg:col-span-3">
        <p class="reveal text-base leading-relaxed text-inverted-foreground/60">
          In the late 19th century, Budapest's cafes were not restaurants. They
          were institutions — writing desks, debate halls, newsrooms, and second
          homes for a generation of writers, journalists, composers, and
          intellectuals who shaped Central European culture from marble-topped
          tables.
        </p>

        <p
          class="reveal mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-inverted-foreground/60"
        >
          Cafe New York, opened in 1894 and called "the most beautiful cafe in
          the world," became so legendary that writers reportedly threw its keys
          into the Danube so it would never close. Cafe Central, Cafe Gerbeaud,
          and dozens of others formed a network of cultural spaces where the
          line between a cup of coffee and a revolution was always thin.
        </p>

        <blockquote
          class="reveal mt-10 border-l-2 border-inverted-foreground/10 pl-6"
        >
          <p
            class="font-display text-headline-3 font-bold italic leading-snug text-inverted-foreground/80"
          >
            "Some cities are remembered by their monuments. Others by the
            conversations that happened inside them."
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p
          class="reveal mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-inverted-foreground/40"
        >
          Today, the tradition continues. Modern roaster cafes sit alongside
          restored 19th-century grand cafes, and the Hungarian word for
          coffeehouse — kavehaz — still carries the weight of an idea: that
          civilization happens one table at a time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
